# Welcome to redis

This service provides a Docker-managed Redis instance for caching and data storage:

- Provides connection string as configuration to dependent services
- Uses the pinned `codeflydev/redis` runtime image
- Supports optional password authentication

This service provides a local Redis instance for development and testing purposes.

## Read replicas

```yaml
endpoints:
  - name: read
    api: tcp
  - name: write
    api: tcp
spec:
  with-read-replicas: true
  read-replicas: 2   # optional, defaults to 1
```

The `write` endpoint (or the only other one) is the primary; `read`, and any
other TCP endpoint, are served by the replicas, which replicate the primary with
the same password and refuse writes. A replica counts as ready only once its link
to the primary is up.

- Configuration: `redis.connection` names the primary, `redis.read-connection`
  the replicas. The `cache` group always names the primary.
- Locally (Docker or nix) a read endpoint has one address, so it reaches the
  first replica; further replicas run, replicate and are held to the same
  readiness on ports of their own, but no local endpoint routes to them.
- Under Docker the primary and its replicas share one container. This is a
  deliberate choice: core's docker runner has no container-to-container
  network, so a replica in a container of its own could not reach the primary.
  One container is also the failure domain the nix runtime has (one host, one
  process set). A replica that dies inside it is not reported; the primary's
  exit is.
- In Kubernetes the primary and the replicas are two StatefulSets behind the
  one Service: the write port reaches only the primary, the read port balances
  across every replica. Replicas keep their data in an `emptyDir` and resync
  from the primary when they restart.

Without `with-read-replicas`, one redis serves every declared endpoint.

## Redis version

The container runtime serves the Redis version baked into the pinned runtime
image (`runtime-image.json`). The Docker-free (nix) runtime serves the Redis
that `nix/flake.lock` pins: `redis-server` is taken from the PATH the locked
devShell exports, never from a `/nix/store` scan or the host's PATH, so the
version only ever moves when that lock does. The two are one version: the flake
pins the nixpkgs revision shipping the image's Redis, and the agent's
real-server suite fails either runtime that reports another.

## Lifecycle

| operation | execution resources | data |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Stop (native) | released — the redis process is terminated and the port freed | retained: the dataset is written to the data directory on shutdown and reloaded by the next Init |
| Stop (native) with `keep-running: true` | retained — the server stays up for reuse | retained; the server keeps serving it and keeps snapshotting on its save point |
| Stop (Docker) | retained — the container stays up for reuse | retained, in the running container |
| Destroy (native) | released | retained on disk, same as Stop |
| Destroy (Docker) | released; the container is removed | **discarded** — the container has no volume, so removing it removes its data |

No operation deletes the native data directory or the generated config. If the
native redis process dies on its own, the agent reports the service as failed
rather than continuing to advertise it as started.

The native runner records Core's stable boot-session and process-birth identity.
It also carries the current launching workspace through Nix materialization
and persists it in Core's versioned ownership record. This survives Redis's
process-title/environment rewriting and the death of the controlling CLI, even
when the data directory is outside the workspace. Cached shell exports cannot
replace that launch marker. Scoped lifecycle commands must consume Core's
ownership discovery and inspection API; older readers ignore the new record
namespace and cannot recover these new runs. Scope metadata does not authorize
signaling: Core still checks the recorded process birth before delivery.

Agents built before this identity update can leave older clock-based records.
Updating the agent affects new runs; it does not rewrite retained evidence or
prove ownership of an already-running old process. Incompatible retained records
require explicit owner recovery, never process-name inference or broad cleanup.
